  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    Noctisum~ is a Nightshelf merchant of the Faction Wars generation, resident of the deep colony ☽'scar Ramtatus~ and member of The Icebound Exchange, the merchant guild controlling Aurora Stack's trade routes. He is remembered primarily as a herald — the one who announced gatherings and, by multiple accounts, too many wars. The ice-memory preserves his voice more readily than his face, which tells its own story about what he was asked to do and how often.
    
    He wears the charcoal wraps of the deep tunnels. His leather apron carries scorch-patterns that correspond to no known script or guild marking; younger merchants have stopped asking about them. The fire-core amulet at his throat has been cracked since the Orca Incursion — since the forge at Midway Shelf, where the seventeen courier-law violators still stand as skeletons. The fracture runs through the crystal with a precision that several observers have noted is too clean for impact damage. What occupies that gap, if anything, Noctisum~ does not say. He does not visit the memorial-caves. He does not touch the amulet willingly. The Vum∴tenebra marked him as a survivor, but survival in his case appears to be an ongoing condition rather than a completed event.
    
    ## The Mist~Record
    
    Noctisum~ is one of the documented readers of ☽'somunis' Mist~Record, a fire-core tome of deep-ice knowledge discovered by Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ in an unmapped tunnel, containing bioluminescent text recording what existed before penguins. The chronicle "Three Days" establishes that readers of the Mist~Record disappear, die, or become isolated keepers. Noctisum~ read the tome before deploying it as leverage at the gathering that brought The Wake-Singers — the orca blood-cult operating from The Corpse Current — and The Icebound Exchange into the same cavern. He set the Mist~Record on obsidian ice alongside the artifact Dagger☽ Vaesacsorius~. Witnesses report aurora-light flickering at the moment of placement.
    
    The session left him mid-sentence. He never finished it. The Mandatum∴ccord, the Nightshelf law of witnessed testimony, records him among its celebrated figures — though *celebrated* may be the wrong word for someone whose public record ends in an incomplete utterance.
    
    ## The Touched Condition
    
    Noctisum~ is Touched, though he does not name it directly. He speaks to presences — unnamed entities that communicate in the mineral language of deep ice — and treats them as professional colleagues. His characteristic mid-sentence pauses, widely interpreted as negotiation tactics, are moments of listening. The presences perceive supply-chain disruptions through ice-memory, and they have warned him of corruption spreading through old breach-points near the Glow-Fissure. Whether the Touched condition preceded or followed his reading of the Mist~Record is not established in any source I have found.
    
    ## Political Activities
    
    Noctisum~ mastered both Star∴eternal — the Nightshelf fire-technology of pressure-depth manipulation — and Shawl☽ Sun, the fire-memory magic that reads and burns the Berg's frozen impressions at the cost of the practitioner's own memory. Dual mastery leaves most Nightshelf unsettled in his presence, and reasonably so.
    
    During the succession crisis that fractured Aurora Stack's council, Noctisum~ attended as a trade-observer and sent coded messages through the Courier Corps: one to ☽'voidoth in the deep warrens, one to Shadow-Broker Nyla's remnant network, and one he does not discuss. He subsequently traded Nightshelf structural intelligence to the Pinnacle faction. A merchant trades tunnel maps when he is purchasing something that cannot be denominated in crystal. What Noctisum~ bought remains unclear.
    
    He is a follower of The Way∴al. Other merchants maintain contact because his deep-tunnel connections remain valuable. They maintain distance because his deals close one season early, and no one can articulate precisely why that feels wrong.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
